## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to integrate 3-valued logic with cardinality constraints in a policy-like setting and to sketch a solver.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic
- Arc-consistency/backtracking with U
- Cardinality constraints and pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch for Kleene tri-valued ops
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function or(a,b){ return order[a] > order[b]? a : b; }
function and(a,b){ return order[a] < order[b]? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to larger rule sets with cycles?
- What is the complexity of finding a satisfying assignment with at least one U?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic in a realistic policy-engine style, including short-circuit semantics and propagation.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: ¬ flips T/F, U→U; ∧/∨ follow min/max with T>U>F; A→B as ¬A ∨ B.
- Short-circuit evaluation in rule engines.
- Backtracking propagation for constraint satisfaction in a 3-valued setting.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(a){ return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ /* min/max with T>U>F */ return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = kleeneNot(a)
  return kleeneOr(na, b)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the answer change if short-circuiting is disabled? 
- How would you extend to detect unsatisfiable sets with more variables?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about constrained, multi-valued logic with propagation and backtracking, mirroring solver internals.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation (T, F, U)
- Horn-like implications
- Backtracking with forward-propagation and early pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch: propagate(rule): if antecedent is T, set consequent to T; if consequent is F, fail
function propagate(state) {
  let changed = false;
  for (const r of rules) {
    if (r.antecedent(state) === true) {
      if (state[r.consequent] !== 'T') { state[r.consequent] = 'T'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return changed;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n variables and cycles while guaranteeing termination?
- How would you implement heuristics to choose which U to branch on first to minimize backtracks?

## Why This Is Asked
Every candidate demonstrates logic reasoning and basic constraint solving relevant to planning validations in software.

## Key Concepts
- Propositional logic and implications (A→B means ¬A ∨ B)
- Satisfiability with small variable sets
- Backtracking with constraint propagation for pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSatisfiable(constraints, numVars = 3) {
  const vars = Array.from({length: numVars}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));

  function backtrack(index, assignment) {
    if (index === numVars) return constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment));

    for (const val of [0, 1]) {
      assignment[vars[index]] = val;
      if (constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment))) {
        if (backtrack(index + 1, assignment)) return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0, {});
}

const constraints = [
  a => a.A === 0 || a.B === 1,
  a => a.B === 0 || a.C === 1,
  a => a.A === 1 || a.C === 1
];
console.log(isSatisfiable(constraints)); // true
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to thousands of variables with CNF clauses?
- What are the trade-offs of backtracking vs. SAT solvers in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small CSP with Kleene semantics, reason about propagation, and craft a concrete counterfactual assignment with U, plus depth-limited reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬ maps T↔F, U↔U.
- Implication as ¬X ∨ Y; F only when antecedent is T and consequent F.
- Depth-limited backtracking with constraint propagation to avoid exploring futile branches.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function kleeneOr(a,b){ return a==='T'||b==='T'?'T': a==='U'||b==='U'?'U':'F';}
function kleeneImp(a,b){ // a -> b
  if(a==='T' && b==='F') return 'F';
  if(a==='T' && b==='U') return 'U';
  if(a==='F') return 'T';
  if(a==='U' && b==='F') return 'U';
  return (b==='T') ? 'T' : 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would adding a 6th variable complicate backtracking?
- How to prove optimality of the search depth?

